<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newcastle upon Tyne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Newcastle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Newcastle upon Tyne is a city in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a>, named after the castle founded in 1080 on the site of the Roman fort of Pons Aelius. It grew rich from exporting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, especially to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, and was a key northern stronghold against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. Scottish Covenanter armies occupied it in 1640 and took it again by storm in 1644; it later became a major industrial and shipbuilding centre.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newcastle-upon-tyne.jpg" alt="Newcastle upon Tyne" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JimmyGuano · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newcastle-upon-Tyne-bridges-and-skyline_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Newcastle upon Tyne stands on the northern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tyne">River Tyne</a>, opposite Gateshead, about 74 kilometres south of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s northernmost city and the most populous settlement of the Tyneside conurbation. It was historically part of the county of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, and since 1974 it has belonged to the metropolitan county of Tyne and Wear. Fragments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall</a> are still visible in parts of the city, especially along the West Road, and the line of the wall can be traced east to the Roman fort of Segedunum at Wallsend.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first recorded settlement was Pons Aelius, a Roman fort and bridge across the Tyne, named after the family name of the emperor Hadrian, who founded it in the second century. After the Roman departure it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of Northumbria and was known as Munucceaster, or Monkchester. Conflicts with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> in 876 left the settlements along the Tyne in ruins, and after the rebellion against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> in 1088 Monkchester was nearly destroyed by Odo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayeux">Bayeux</a>. The settlement took its later name from a castle built in 1080 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-curthose">Robert Curthose</a>, the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a>.</p><p>Throughout the Middle Ages Newcastle was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s northern fortress. A stone wall 7.6 metres high was built round the town in the thirteenth century to defend it in the border war against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. The Scottish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-lion">William the Lion</a> was imprisoned there in 1174, and the town was successfully defended against the Scots three times in the fourteenth century. In 1400 Henry IV separated it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> and made it a county in itself. A royal act of 1530 restricted all shipments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> from Tyneside to Newcastle Quayside, giving a monopoly to a cartel of burgesses known as the Hostmen, and this helped the town prosper. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> granted a new charter in 1589.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> it became one of the world's largest shipbuilding and repair centres. Electric trams were introduced in 1901 and gradually replaced by trolley buses from 1935, with the tram service ending in 1950. The Tyne and Wear Metro, opened in five phases between 1980 and 1984, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s first urban light rail system.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Newcastle is a metropolitan borough with city status, governed by Newcastle City Council, and the largest settlement of North East England. The Anglican St Nicholas' Cathedral has a lantern tower dating from 1474, and the city has two other cathedrals, St Mary's, designed by Augustus Welby Pugin, and the Coptic Orthodox Cathedral. Its landmarks include the Tyne Bridge, the Swing Bridge and the castle. The Great North Run starts here, and Newcastle United has played at St James' Park since 1892.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Newcastle upon Tyne” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 2021 workers found a previously undiscovered 3-metre section in central <b>Newcastle upon Tyne</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Newcastle</b> United supporters sang it in the last minutes of the 1955 FA Cup Final, and in 1997 military bands played it as British representatives left Hong Kong on HMY Britannia after the handover.</blockquote><a href="/songs/now-is-the-hour">Now Is the Hour</a></li><li><blockquote>The earliest known publications of the words are an illustrated children's book, Mirth Without Mischief, published in London in 1780, and a broadsheet by Angus of <b>Newcastle</b>, dated to the late 18th or early 19th century; their words are almost identical, and neither has music.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-twelve-days-of-christmas">The Twelve Days of Christmas</a></li></ul></section>
